+## Conversion bodies (#1775)
+
+An erased variant wrapper does not erase arbitrary From code. The conversion
+owner, lower/untagged/conversions.rs, distinguishes identity wrappers from real
+bodies. From and Into resolve the same implementation. Calls and borrow/effect
+analysis consume that decision, and a library publishes real bodies so private
+state remains owned there. Local identity wrappers are proved from their body;
+foreign wrappers need available MIR or the existing identity binding contract.
+WebAPI payload conversion generators use link_name=this. Native comparisons cover
+owned strings, numeric side effects and live borrow checks; consumers cover both
+real bodies with private state and pure wrappers without inline hints.
+
+This is ADR 0262's results-change case: conversion effects are preserved. The
+old refusal of non-wrapper implementations is removed. Mutations erase a real
+body, assume its effects away, omit library publication and discard a pure
+wrapper proof.
+
+The broad downstream gate additionally checks existing metadata-only React and
+Next bindings. Their nine payload wrapper From methods use the existing This
+identity contract, including the opaque ref handle and callback (whose Box is
+the JS function). This is explicit only at the JavaScript boundary; application
+conversion bodies are neither assumed pure nor erased. Standard From/Into
+resolution is recognition/conversions.rs; the untagged owner only proves and
+classifies the body it is given. The former non-wrapper refusal mutation is
+retired because that refusal no longer exists, replaced by real-body behavior
+mutations; remaining method reachability mutations follow their new sites.
